<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbas I of Persia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shah Abbas I, Abbas I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abbas I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, known as Shah Abbas the Great, was the most celebrated ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, reigning from 1588 to 1629. He reorganised the army with slave soldiers (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulams</a>) and firearms, curbed the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>, recovered territory from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>, and moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. His reign marked the political, economic and artistic high point of Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia.jpg" alt="Abbas I of Persia" width="286" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bishandas (attribution) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:12_Abu%27l_Hasan_Jahangir_Welcoming_Shah_%27Abbas,_ca._1618,_Freer_Gallery_of_Art,_Washington_DC_(portrait).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
